course: dictionary
lesson: 7018
title: Parliamentary
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Parliamentary means to do with a parliament, the group of elected people who make the laws. The ending -ary means "connected with", so a parliamentary seat is a seat in a parliament. You will see it in phrases like «parliamentary democracy» and «parliamentary candidate».
explain: The sentence «When the Commons and the Lords disagree, a bill goes back and forth until they agree» gives you clues. The Commons and the Lords are two parts of a parliament, and a bill is a plan for a new law. The phrase parliamentary ping-pong names this back-and-forth, so it is about how a parliament works.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it in. «She is a parliamentary candidate» becomes «She is a candidate connected with a parliament», which makes sense. If the swap sounds silly, your meaning is wrong, so look at the clues again.
fact: «Parliamentary» means to do with a parliament, or done by a parliament.
fact: A parliament is a group of elected people who make laws.
fact: A parliamentary democracy is a country where elected people in a parliament help run the country.
fact: The UK, Ireland, Canada, Australia and New Zealand have parliamentary systems.
fact: In a parliamentary system, the government is led by the party with the most seats in parliament.
exercise: «She is a parliamentary candidate, so she wants to be elected to parliament.» What does parliamentary mean here? | Very quiet | To do with a school playground | Made of paper | *To do with a parliament
exercise: In «parliamentary democracy», what does parliamentary tell you? | The country is very small | *The country is run with a parliament | The people are very rich | The country has no leaders at all
exercise: «When the Commons and the Lords disagree, a bill goes back and forth until they agree.» Find and copy one word that names part of a parliament. | agree | back | *Commons | bill
test: What does «parliamentary seat» mean? | A seat at the theatre | *A seat in a parliament | A seat on a bus | A chair in a classroom
test: Which countries have parliamentary systems, according to this lesson? | Only France | Only the UK, and no others | *The UK, Ireland, Canada, Australia and New Zealand | Only Japan
exam: What is «parliamentary ping-pong»? | *A bill going back and forth between the Commons and the Lords until they agree | A game played in the Lords | A rule about sports | A bill that is passed in one day
